s in the namespace for any related errors. \n\nAnother approach: since the frontend-proxy is in Terminating state, maybe there\'s a rollout or update that caused it to be terminated. Checking the deployment for frontend-proxy might show if there\'s a new version being rolled out, leading to the old pod being terminated. Also, checking the service\'s endpoints to see if the frontend-proxy is still listed there. If the service is not pointing to any endpoints, that could indicate a problem. \n\nBut the user\'s available tools include exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd, so maybe they can check the deployment or replica set for frontend-proxy. For example, using kubectl get deployment frontend-proxy to see the status, or check the events related to the deployment. Alternatively, check the pods that are currently running for frontend-proxy to see if there\'s a new pod that\'s taking over. \n\nWait, the user\'s previous command to get pods showed that frontend-proxy-84788b44b-gtgdz is in Terminating, and there\'s another pod frontend-proxy-6df8c768f5-pj4kn running. So maybe the deployment is rolling out a new version, and the old pod is being terminated. But why would that cause a problem? If the new pod is not healthy, that could be an issue. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the frontend-proxy is part of a service that\'s not functioning correctly, leading to errors in other services. But since the frontend is running, maybe the issue is with the frontend-proxy\'s termination. \n\nSo, the next step is to check the deployment for frontend-proxy to see if there are any issues with the rollout. Using kubectl get deployment frontend-proxy -n astronomy-shop to check the status. If there\'s a rollout in progress, that could explain the terminating pod. Also, checking the events for the deployment might show why the pod was terminated. \n\nAlternatively, check the service\'s endpoints to see if the frontend-proxy is still accessible. If the service is not pointing to any endpoints, that could indicate a problem. \n\nBut given the available tools, the user can run kubectl commands. So, the next tool call should be to check the deployment status of frontend-proxy. Let\'s use exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd with "kubectl get deployment frontend-proxy -n astronomy-shop" to see the deployment status and any rollout issues.\n</think>\n\nexec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \nThe frontend-proxy pod is in a "Terminating" state, but the describe command failed. To investigate further, I will check the deployment status of `frontend-proxy` to determine if there is an ongoing rollout, scaling, or error causing the pod to terminate unexpectedly. This will help identify if the termination is intentional or due to a fault.  \n\n<tool_call>\n{"name": "exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd", "arguments": {"command": "kubectl get deployment frontend-proxy -n astronomy-shop"}}\n</tool_call>', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 898, 'prompt_tokens': 3350, 'total_tokens': 4248, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfbfa-4495-7fa0-ac20-a89251a4fa3d-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 3350, 'output_tokens': 898, 'total_tokens': 4248}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='c0516319-3c45-45d3-849f-26a2063c2d67'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose an issue with the frontend-proxy pod in the astronomy-shop namespace. The pod is in a Terminating state, and previous attempts to get its logs or describe it resulted in errors. \n\nFirst, I called `kubectl get pods` and saw that frontend-proxy-84788b44b-gtgdz is Terminating. Then, I tried to get its logs but got a "Not Found" error, which might be because the pod is already gone. Next, I tried to describe the pod, but that also failed. So, the pod might have been deleted or is in the process of being terminated.\n\nNow, the user is suggesting checking the deployment status of frontend-proxy. The next step is to use `kubectl get deployment frontend-proxy -n astronomy-shop` to see the deployment details. This will show the number of replicas, desired replicas, and any rollout history. If the deployment is scaling down or there\'s a rollout error, that could explain why the pod is terminating. \n\nBy checking the deployment, we can determine if there\'s an active rollout, scaling action, or if the pod was terminated due to a failure. This will help identify if the termination is intentional (like a rollback or scaling down) or if there\'s an underlying issue causing the pod to crash. \n\nSo, the next tool call should be to get the deployment details. If the deployment shows an error or an unexpected change, that\'s a clue. If not, maybe check the service or other related resources. But first, the deployment info is crucial here.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get deployment frontend-proxy -n astronomy-shop"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-bb037d87cddc399d', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 363, 'prompt_tokens': 4363, 'total_tokens': 4726, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfbfa-6e2e-7ea3-8d21-1c049aca7fa2-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl get deployment frontend-proxy -n astronomy-shop'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-bb037d87cddc399d', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_ca