ional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 285, 'prompt_tokens': 3582, 'total_tokens': 3867, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfbfa-817e-7bc3-8e65-681005255d71-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 3582, 'output_tokens': 285, 'total_tokens': 3867}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='c9360512-586d-4e61-9ba2-472eebd406a4'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let me try to figure out what's going on here. The user is trying to diagnose an issue with the frontend-proxy service in the astronomy-shop namespace. From the previous steps, we saw that the frontend-proxy pod was in a Terminating state, but when they tried to describe it, it wasn't found. Then, checking the deployment showed that the frontend-proxy deployment has 1/1 replicas ready, which seems normal. \n\nNow, the user is looking at the service definition for frontend-proxy. The service is supposed to route traffic to the pods, so if there's a misconfiguration here, that could cause issues. The next step is to check the service's YAML to see if there are any problems. \n\nWait, the user just called `kubectl get service frontend-proxy -n astronomy-shop -o yaml` and got a response. Let me check the response to see what's in there. The service YAML would include details like the selector, ports, and type. If the selector doesn't match the pod's labels, the service wouldn't route traffic correctly, leading to the pod being terminated or not being accessible. \n\nLooking at the service's YAML, if the selector is incorrect, that's a problem. For example, if the service is looking for a label like `app: frontend-proxy` but the pod has a different label, the service wouldn't find the pods, leading to termination. Also, checking the ports to ensure they match the container's exposed ports is important. \n\nAnother thing to check is the service type. If it's set to ClusterIP but the pods are not reachable, that could cause issues. Or if there's a misconfiguration in the endpoints. \n\nOnce the service YAML is reviewed, if any discrepancies are found, that would be the root cause. If not, maybe the issue is elsewhere, like in the deployment configuration or network policies. But since the pod was terminating, the service misconfiguration is a likely candidate. \n\nSo, the next step after getting the service YAML is to analyze it for any mismatches or errors. If everything looks good, then the problem might be in another area, like the pod's own configuration or external factors. But based on the current information, checking the service's YAML is the logical next step.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get service frontend-proxy -n astronomy-shop -o yaml"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-9e13c0ce6cee7111', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 501, 'prompt_tokens': 4574, 'total_tokens': 5075, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfbfa-8ed4-7e80-89f3-1825e4b4d61e-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl get service frontend-proxy -n astronomy-shop -o yaml'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-9e13c0ce6cee7111', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 4574, 'output_tokens': 501, 'total_tokens': 5075}), ToolMessage(content='apiVersion: v1\nkind: Service\nmetadata:\n  annotations:\n    meta.helm.sh/release-name: astronomy-shop\n    meta.helm.sh/release-namespace: astronomy-shop\n  creationTimestamp: "2026-03-17T13:23:34Z"\n  labels:\n    app.kubernetes.io/component: frontend-proxy\n    app.kubernetes.io/managed-by: Helm\n    app.kubernetes.io/name: frontend-proxy\n    app.kubernetes.io/part-of: opentelemetry-demo\n    app.kubernetes.io/version: 2.2.0\n    helm.sh/chart: opentelemetry-demo-0.40.4\n    opentelemetry.io/name: frontend-proxy\n  name: frontend-proxy\n  namespace: astronomy-shop\n  resourceVersion: "181847"\n  uid: a0381bf8-cc2c-4b59-a34a-87cfcf859afe\nspec:\n  clusterIP: 10.104.60.144\n  clusterIPs:\n  - 10.104.60.144\n  internalTrafficPolicy: Cluster\n  ipFamilies:\n  - IPv4\n  ipFamilyPolicy: SingleStack\n  ports:\n  - name: tcp-service\n    port: 8080\n    protocol: TCP\n    targetPort: 8080\n  selector:\n    opentelemetry.io/name: frontend-proxy\n  sessionAffinity: None\n  type: ClusterIP\nstatus:\n  loadBalancer: {}\n', id='78464d71-208a-4cd9-9dbb-6d6c5e70f82a', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-9e13c0ce6cee7111'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='5f6d128b-25cc-4b88-8530-a3393b513777'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose an issue with the OpenTelemetry Demo Astronomy Shop application. So far, they\'ve checked the services, deployments, and pods. The frontend-proxy pod is in a Terminating state, but the deployment shows it\'s up-to-date. The service configuration for frontend-proxy seems correct, with the right selector and port.\n\nWait, maybe the problem is with the pod\'s termination. Since the pod is terminating but the deployment is healthy, perhaps there\'s an issue with the pod itself. The user tried to get the pod\'s logs but got a "not found" error, which might be because the pod is already gone. But they can still check the pod\'s status or events for more details.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the service isn\'t properly routing traffic, leading to the frontend-proxy pod being terminated. Or maybe there\'s a network policy blocking communication. The user could check the events related to the frontend-proxy pod to see if there were any errors during its lifecycle. Also, checking the network policies in the namespace might help identify if there are any restrictions affecting the pod.\n\nAnother angle: since the frontend-proxy is a proxy service, maybe it\'s not forwarding traffic correctly, causing upstream services to fail, which in turn affects the frontend. But the frontend\'s traces didn\'t show any issues. Hmm.\n\nWait, the user should check the pod\'s events to see why it\'s terminating. They can use kubectl describe pod on the frontend-proxy, but since the pod is already terminated, maybe the events are still available. Alternatively, check the deployment\'s rollout history to see if there was a recent update that caused the termination.\n\nAlso, looking at the service\'s endpoints to ensure that the frontend-proxy pod is listed there. If the service isn\'t pointing to any pods, that could cause issues. The user can check the endpoints for the frontend-proxy service.\n\nAnother possibility is that the pod is being terminated due to resource constraints. Checking the resource usage metrics for the frontend-proxy pod might reveal if it\'s being evicted due to OOM or CPU limits. Using get_metrics with appropriate PromQL queries could help here.\n\nBut given the tools available, the next step would be to check the pod\'s events to get more details on why it\'s terminating. Since the pod is already gone, the events might still be accessible through kubectl describe pod, but if that\'s not possible, maybe checking the deployment\'s status or the service\'s endpoints would be better. Alternatively, looking into the network policies or ingress rules that might affect the frontend-proxy\'s operation.\n</think>\n\nexec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \nThe frontend-proxy pod is in a "Terminating" state, but the deployment and service configurations appear correct. To determine why the pod is terminating, I will check the pod\'s events for errors or warnings that might indicate crashes, evictions, or misconfigurations. This will help identify if the termination is due to a fault in the pod itself or external factors.  \n\n<tool_call>\n{"name": "exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd", "arguments": {"command": "kubectl describe pod frontend-proxy-84788b44b-gtgdz -n astronomy-shop"}}\n</tool_call>', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 653, 'prompt_tokens': 4108, 'total_tokens': 4761, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfbfa-a8ac-7d83-bb5f-72a88a36cc79-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 4108, 'output_tokens': 653, 'total_tokens': 4761}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='965284bd-290a-41ec-8628-8c9e20a172ff'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's see. The user is trying to diagnose why the frontend-proxy pod is in a Terminating state. They've already checked the deployment, service, and pod logs, but the describe command isn't working. Maybe the pod was already deleted, so the describe command can't find it. The next step is to check the pod's events or maybe look into the deployment's rollout history. Alternatively, checking the node where the pod was running might show if there were any issues. But since the pod isn't found, perhaps the deployment is rolling out a new version, and the old pod is being terminated. They should check the deployment's status and maybe the rollout history to see if there's a pending update causing the termination. Also, checking the namespace for any events related to the frontend-proxy could help. If the deployment is stuck, maybe there's a problem with the replicas or resource limits. They might need to look into the deployment's YAML to see if there are any misconfigurations. Alternatively, using the get_events command in the namespace could reveal any recent issues affecting the pod.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get events -n astronomy-shop --field-selector involvedObject.name=frontend-proxy"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-a2d83decb6579e39', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 265, 'prompt_tokens': 5140, 'total_tokens': 5405, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfbfa-c746-7e83-9a0f-b012a64b3a04-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl get events -n astronomy-shop --field-selector involvedObject.name=frontend-proxy'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-a2d83decb6579e39', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 5140, 'output_tokens': 265, 'total_tokens': 5405}), ToolMessage(content='LAST SEEN   TYPE     REASON              OBJECT                      MESSAGE\n3m41s       Normal   ScalingReplicaSet   deployment/frontend-proxy   Scaled up replica set frontend-proxy-84788b44b to 1\n104s        Normal   ScalingReplicaSet   deployment/frontend-proxy   Scaled up replica set frontend-proxy-6df8c768f5 to 1\n', id='267a72bd-428c-4d1f-805a-66740af66814', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-a2d83decb6579e39'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='17da7f2b-cbd2-4516-9ee6-c1905d4f03ea')]]
