 is already gone. But they can still check the pod\'s status or events for more details.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the service isn\'t properly routing traffic, leading to the frontend-proxy pod being terminated. Or maybe there\'s a network policy blocking communication. The user could check the events related to the frontend-proxy pod to see if there were any errors during its lifecycle. Also, checking the network policies in the namespace might help identify if there are any restrictions affecting the pod.\n\nAnother angle: since the frontend-proxy is a proxy service, maybe it\'s not forwarding traffic correctly, causing upstream services to fail, which in turn affects the frontend. But the frontend\'s traces didn\'t show any issues. Hmm.\n\nWait, the user should check the pod\'s events to see why it\'s terminating. They can use kubectl describe pod on the frontend-proxy, but since the pod is already terminated, maybe the events are still available. Alternatively, check the deployment\'s rollout history to see if there was a recent update that caused the termination.\n\nAlso, looking at the service\'s endpoints to ensure that the frontend-proxy pod is listed there. If the service isn\'t pointing to any pods, that could cause issues. The user can check the endpoints for the frontend-proxy service.\n\nAnother possibility is that the pod is being terminated due to resource constraints. Checking the resource usage metrics for the frontend-proxy pod might reveal if it\'s being evicted due to OOM or CPU limits. Using get_metrics with appropriate PromQL queries could help here.\n\nBut given the tools available, the next step would be to check the pod\'s events to get more details on why it\'s terminating. Since the pod is already gone, the events might still be accessible through kubectl describe pod, but if that\'s not possible, maybe checking the deployment\'s status or the service\'s endpoints would be better. Alternatively, looking into the network policies or ingress rules that might affect the frontend-proxy\'s operation.\n</think>\n\nexec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \nThe frontend-proxy pod is in a "Terminating" state, but the deployment and service configurations appear correct. To determine why the pod is terminating, I will check the pod\'s events for errors or warnings that might indicate crashes, evictions, or misconfigurations. This will help identify if the termination is due to a fault in the pod itself or external factors.  \n\n<tool_call>\n{"name": "exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd", "arguments": {"command": "kubectl describe pod frontend-proxy-84788b44b-gtgdz -n astronomy-shop"}}\n</tool_call>', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 653, 'prompt_tokens': 4108, 'total_tokens': 4761, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfbfa-a8ac-7d83-bb5f-72a88a36cc79-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 4108, 'output_tokens': 653, 'total_tokens': 4761}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='965284bd-290a-41ec-8628-8c9e20a172ff'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's see. The user is trying to diagnose why the frontend-proxy pod is in a Terminating state. They've already checked the deployment, service, and pod logs, but the describe command isn't working. Maybe the pod was already deleted, so the describe command can't find it. The next step is to check the pod's events or maybe look into the deployment's rollout history. Alternatively, checking the node where the pod was running might show if there were any issues. But since the pod isn't found, perhaps the deployment is rolling out a new version, and the old pod is being terminated. They should chec