s in the namespace for any related errors. \n\nAnother approach: since the frontend-proxy is in Terminating state, maybe there\'s a rollout or update that caused it to be terminated. Checking the deployment for frontend-proxy might show if there\'s a new version being rolled out, leading to the old pod being terminated. Also, checking the service\'s endpoints to see if the frontend-proxy is still listed there. If the service is not pointing to any endpoints, that could indicate a problem. \n\nBut the user\'s available tools include exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd, so maybe they can check the deployment or replica set for frontend-proxy. For example, using kubectl get deployment frontend-proxy to see the status, or check the events related to the deployment. Alternatively, check the pods that are currently running for frontend-proxy to see if there\'s a new pod that\'s taking over. \n\nWait, the user\'s previous command to get pods showed that frontend-proxy-84788b44b-gtgdz is in Terminating, and there\'s another pod frontend-proxy-6df8c768f5-pj4kn running. So maybe the deployment is rolling out a new version, and the old pod is being terminated. But why would that cause a problem? If the new pod is not healthy, that could be an issue. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the frontend-proxy is part of a service that\'s not functioning correctly, leading to errors in other services. But since the frontend is running, maybe the issue is with the frontend-proxy\'s termination. \n\nSo, the next step is to check the deployment for frontend-proxy to see if there are any issues with the rollout. Using kubectl get deployment frontend-proxy -n astronomy-shop to check the status. If there\'s a rollout in progress, that could explain the terminating pod. Also, checking the events for the deployment might show why the pod was terminated. \n\nAlternatively, check the service\'s endpoints to see if the frontend-proxy is still accessible. If the service is not pointing to any endpoints, that could 