r events for more details.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the service isn\'t properly routing traffic, leading to the frontend-proxy pod being terminated. Or maybe there\'s a network policy blocking communication. The user could check the events related to the frontend-proxy pod to see if there were any errors during its lifecycle. Also, checking the network policies in the namespace might help identify if there are any restrictions affecting the pod.\n\nAnother angle: since the frontend-proxy is a proxy service, maybe it\'s not forwarding traffic correctly, causing upstream services to fail, which in turn affects the frontend. But the frontend\'s traces didn\'t show any issues. Hmm.\n\nWait, the user should check the pod\'s events to see why it\'s terminating. They can use kubectl describe pod on the frontend-proxy, but since the pod is already terminated, maybe the events are still available. Alternatively, check the deployment\'s rollout history to see if there was a recent update that caused the termination.\n\nAlso, looking at the service\'s endpoints to ensure that the frontend-proxy pod is listed there. If the service isn\'t pointing to any pods, that could cause issues. The user can check the endpoints for the frontend-proxy service.\n\nAnother possibility is that the pod is being terminated due to resource constraints. Checking the resource usage metrics for the frontend-proxy pod might reveal if it\'s being evicted due to OOM or CPU limits. Using get_metrics with appropriate PromQL queries could help here.\n\nBut given the tools available, the next step would be to check the pod\'s events to get more details on why it\'s terminating. Since the pod is already gone, the events might still be accessible through kubectl describe pod, but if that\'s not possible, maybe checking the deployment\'s status or the service\'s endpoints would be better. Alternatively, looking into the network policies or ingress rules that might affect the frontend-proxy\'s operation.\n</think>\n\nexec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \nThe frontend-proxy pod is in a "Terminating" state, but the deployment and service configurations appear correct. To determine why the pod is terminating, I will check the pod\'s events for errors or warnings that might indicate crashes, evictions, or misconfigurations. This will help identify if the termination is due to a fault in the pod itself or external factors.  \n\n<tool_call>\n{"name": "exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd", "arguments": {"command": "kubectl describe pod frontend-proxy-84788b44b-gtgdz -n astronomy-shop"}}\n</tool_call>', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 653, 'prompt_tokens': 4108, 'total_tokens': 4761, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfbfa-a8ac-7d83-bb5f-72a88a36cc79-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 4108, 'output_tokens': 653, 'total_tokens': 4761}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='965284bd-290a-41ec-8628-8c9e20a172ff'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's see. The user is trying to diagnose why the frontend-proxy pod is in a Terminating state. They've already checked the deployment, service, and pod logs, but the describe command isn't working. Maybe the pod was already deleted, so the describe command can't find it. The next step is to check the pod's events or maybe look into the deployment's rollout history. Alternatively, checking the node where the pod was running might show if there were any issues. But since the pod isn't found, perhaps the deployment is rolling out a new version, and the old pod is being terminated. They should check the deployment's status and maybe the rollout history to see if there's a pending update causing the termination. Also, checking the namespace for any events related to the frontend-proxy could help. If the deployment is stuck, maybe there's a problem with the replicas or resource limits. They might need to look into the deployment's YAML to see if there are any misconfigurations. Alternatively, using the get_events command in the namespace could reveal any recent issues affecting the pod.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get events -n astronomy-shop --field-selector involvedObject.name=frontend-proxy"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-a2d83decb6579e39', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 265, 'prompt_tokens': 5140, 'total_tokens': 5405, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfbfa-c746-7e83-9a0f-b012a64b3a04-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl get events -n astronomy-shop --field-selector involvedObject.name=frontend-proxy'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-a2d83decb6579e39', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 5140, 'output_tokens': 265, 'total_tokens': 5405}), ToolMessage(content='LAST SEEN   TYPE     REASON              OBJECT                      MESSAGE\n3m41s       Normal   ScalingReplicaSet   deployment/frontend-proxy   Scaled up replica set frontend-proxy-84788b44b to 1\n104s        Normal   ScalingReplicaSet   deployment/frontend-proxy   Scaled up replica set frontend-proxy-6df8c768f5 to 1\n', id='267a72bd-428c-4d1f-805a-66740af66814', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-a2d83decb6579e39'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='17da7f2b-cbd2-4516-9ee6-c1905d4f03ea'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s try to figure out why the frontend-proxy pod is terminating. So far, the user has checked the pods, deployments, service configurations, and events. The pod is in a Terminating state, but the deployment and service look correct. The events show scaling actions but no errors.\n\nHmm, maybe the pod is being terminated due to a rollout or a scaling action. The user tried to describe the pod but got a Not Found error, which might be because the pod is already terminating. The latest events mention scaling up replica sets, which could mean a rollout is happening. \n\nWait, the user ran `kubectl get events` with the field selector for frontend-proxy. The output shows scaling events but no errors. Maybe the pod is being replaced as part of a deployment rollout. The frontend-proxy deployment has two replicas? Wait, earlier when they checked the deployment, it showed 1/1 READY. But the pod name had two replicas? Wait, no, the deployment\'s READY was 1/1, but there were two pods listed: frontend-proxy-84788b44b-gtgdz (Terminating) and frontend-proxy-6df8c768f5-pj4kn (Running). So maybe a new replica was created, and the old one is being terminated. \n\nBut why is the termination causing an issue? If the deployment is rolling out, maybe the new pod isn\'t healthy, or there\'s a configuration issue. Alternatively, maybe the pod is being terminated due to a liveness probe failure. The user hasn\'t checked the pod\'s logs yet. Wait, earlier when they tried to get logs for the terminating pod, it wasn\'t found. But maybe checking the logs of the running pod could show if there\'s an issue. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the service is misconfigured. The service\'s selector is based on opentelemetry.io/name: frontend-proxy. The pod\'s labels should match that. Let me check the pod\'s labels. Wait, the user hasn\'t checked the pod\'s labels yet. If the service selector doesn\'t match the pod\'s labels, the service wouldn\'t route traffic properly, but that might not cause the pod to terminate. \n\nWait, the pod is terminating, but the deployment is healthy. Maybe the termination is part of a normal rollout. However, if the frontend-proxy is critical, its termination could affect other services. The user should check if there are any errors in the pod\'s logs before it terminated. Since the pod is already terminating, maybe the logs are no longer available. \n\nAlternatively, maybe there\'s a resource issue, like memory or CPU limits. The user hasn\'t checked the resource usage. Using `kubectl top pod` could show if the pod was exceeding limits. But the user hasn\'t done that yet. \n\nWait, the user has a tool called `get_metrics` which can query Prometheus. Maybe they can check if there are any metrics related to the frontend-proxy pod, like error rates or resource usage. \n\nBut given the tools available, the next step would be to check the pod\'s logs before it terminated. However, since the pod is already gone, maybe the logs are not available. Alternatively, check the deployment\'s rollout history to see if there were any failures. \n\nWait, the user can use `kubectl rollout history` on the deployment to see if there were any failed rollouts. That might indicate if the termination was due to a deployment issue. \n\nSo, the next tool call should be to check the deployment\'s rollout history. Let me see, the available tools include `exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd`, so the command would be `kubectl rollout history deployment/frontend-proxy -n astronomy-shop`.\n</think>\n\n<tool_call>\n{"name": "exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd", "arguments": {"command": "kubectl rollout history deployment/frontend-proxy -n astronomy-shop"}}\n</tool_call>', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 791, 'prompt_tokens': 4421, 'total_tokens': 5212, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfbfa-d6ca-7181-bb22-bce14f466714-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 4421, 'output_tokens': 791, 'total_tokens': 5212}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='5c0bec25-a8fd-4e0a-b2f2-ab88988bb305'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's see. The user is trying to diagnose why the frontend-proxy pod is in a Terminating state. So far, they checked the pods, deployments, service configs, and events. The deployment shows it's up-to-date, the service seems correctly configured, and the events mention scaling replica sets but no errors.\n\nHmm, maybe there's an issue with the pod's rollout history. The user just ran `kubectl rollout history deployment/frontend-proxy -n astronomy-shop`. The response from the tool call wasn't provided yet, but assuming it's available, checking the rollout history can show if there were any failed deployments or rollbacks. If there's a mismatch between the desired and current replicas, that could cause pods to terminate. Also, looking at the rollout history might reveal if a previous rollout failed, leading to the current state. If the history shows a rollback, that might indicate a problem with the latest deployment. Alternatively, if there are multiple revisions, maybe the pod is stuck in a previous version. The user needs to check the rollout history to see if there are any anomalies or errors in the deployment process that could explain the Terminating state of the pod. If the rollout is healthy, then the issue might be elsewhere, like resource constraints or configuration issues. But based on the previous steps, the main lead is the rollout history.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl rollout history deployment/frontend-proxy -n astronomy-shop"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-8403f93a3c2a8fbd', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 308, 'prompt_tokens': 5358, 'total_tokens': 5666, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfbfa-fbd6-7fc1-9469-cc2644f24026-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl rollout history deployment/frontend-proxy -n astronomy-shop'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-8403f93a3c2a8fbd', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 5358, 'output_tokens': 308, 'total_tokens': 5666}), ToolMessage(content="Your command kubectl rollout history deployment/frontend-proxy -n astronomy-shop is not a read-only kubectl command. Available Read-only Commands: ['kubectl api-resources', 'kubectl api-version', 'kubectl attach', 'kubectl auth can-i', 'kubectl cluster-info', 'kubectl describe', 'kubectl diff', 'kubectl events', 'kubectl explain', 'kubectl get', 'kubectl logs', 'kubectl options', 'kubectl top', 'kubectl version', 'kubectl config view', 'kubectl config current-context', 'kubectl config get'].", id='a1e59835-b122-4b85-886d-eb3bf01e54ae', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-8403f93a3c2a8fbd'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='e25af516-aa53-4de9-ad28-a1a78304e544')]]
