asn\'t checked the service\'s endpoints yet. The service\'s endpoints might not be correctly pointing to the pods. So checking the endpoints could help. \n\nThe available tools include \'get_services\' from Grafana, but the user already listed the services. Maybe checking the service\'s endpoints with kubectl get endpoints. But the user can\'t use \'kubectl get endpoints\' directly. Wait, the available read-only commands include \'kubectl get\', so they can run \'kubectl get endpoints frontend-proxy -n astronomy-shop\'. That would show if the service has endpoints. If there are no endpoints, it might indicate a problem with the service\'s selector or pod labels. \n\nSo the next step is to check the endpoints for the frontend-proxy service to see if it\'s correctly pointing to the pods. If there\'s no endpoint, that could be the root cause. Let me check that.\n</think>\n\nexec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \nTo verify if the frontend-proxy service is correctly routing traffic to its pods, I will check the endpoints associated with the service. If there are no endpoints, it could indicate a misconfiguration in the service\'s selector or pod labels, preventing proper communication.  \n\n<tool_call>\n{"name": "exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd", "arguments": {"command": "kubectl get endpoints frontend-proxy -n astronomy-shop"}}\n</tool_call>', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 767, 'prompt_tokens': 4650, 'total_tokens': 5417, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfbfb-0a7d-7eb0-b19b-3ef99dcec3fb-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 4650, 'output_tokens': 767, 'total_tokens': 5417}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='e53a5bea-7910-46bc-8cec-5be6e289ad70'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's see. The user is trying to diagnose an issue with the frontend-proxy service in the astronomy-shop namespace. The frontend-proxy pod is in a Terminating state, and they've already checked the deployment, service, and events but haven't found the root cause.\n\nFirst, they checked the pods and deployments, which showed the frontend-proxy deployment is healthy with 1/1 replicas. The service configuration looks correct with the right selector and port. The events didn't show any errors, just scaling actions. The rollout history wasn't accessible because the command wasn't read-only.\n\nNow, the user is checking the endpoints for the frontend-proxy service. If there are no endpoints, that would mean the service isn't able to find any pods matching its selector. That could be due to a label mismatch or pods not being ready. But the previous check on the pods showed that the frontend-proxy pod is in Terminating state, which might mean it's being deleted, but there's another pod (frontend-proxy-6df8c768f5) running. Wait, the user previously tried to describe the terminating pod and got an error, but the deployment shows it's scaled up to 1. Maybe there's a rollout in progress?\n\nWait, the user tried to get the rollout history but the command wasn't allowed. So maybe they need to check the current pods again. The user already ran 'kubectl get pods' and saw that frontend-proxy-84788b44b-gtgdz is Terminating, and another pod frontend-proxy-6df8c768f5-pj4kn is Running. So the service's endpoints should point to the running pod. If the endpoints are not showing anything, that might indicate a problem with the service's selector. Let me check the service YAML again. The service's selector is opentelemetry.io/name: frontend-proxy. The pod's labels need to match that. If the pod's labels don't include that, the service won't find it. But earlier, when they checked the service, the selector was correct. Maybe the pod was deleted and the new one hasn't been properly labeled? Or maybe there's a typo in the labels. Alternatively, maybe the service is not properly configured to handle the current pods. \n\nBut the user is now checking the endpoints. If the endpoints are empty, that would confirm the service isn't finding any pods. So the next step is to check the endpoints. If there are no endpoints, then the issue is with the service's selector or pod labels. If there are endpoints, then maybe the pod is terminating, causing temporary issues. But since the pod is in Terminating state, maybe the service is still pointing to it, but the endpoint is being removed. However, the other pod is running, so the endpoints should reflect that. \n\nWait, the user's last command was to get the endpoints, and the response might show if there are any. If there are no endpoints, that's a problem. If there are, then the service is correctly pointing to the running pod. But the frontend-proxy pod is in Terminating, which might be causing some disruption. However, the deployment is scaled to 1, so the other pod should be handling traffic. \n\nSo the next step is to check the endpoints. If there are no endpoints, then the service can't route traffic, leading to issues. If there are endpoints, then the problem might be with the terminating pod. But since the user is trying to find the root cause, checking the endpoints will help determine if the service is correctly configured to find the pods. If the endpoints are missing, that's a configuration issue. If they are present, then the terminating pod might be a red herring, and the real issue is elsewhere. \n\nSo the user should run 'kubectl get endpoints frontend-proxy -n astronomy-shop' to check. If there are no endpoints, then the service's selector is incorrect. If there are endpoints, then the terminating pod might be part of a rollout or a scaling action, but the service is still functioning. \n\nBut the user already checked the deployment and service, so maybe the endpoints are correct. However, the terminating pod might be causing some issues. Alternatively, maybe the frontend-proxy service is not the root cause, and the issue is elsewhere. \n\nIn any case, checking the endpoints is a logical next step to confirm the service's configuration and ensure that it's correctly pointing to the running pods. If the endpoints are correct, then the problem might be with the terminating pod or another service. If not, then the service configuration needs to be adjusted.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get endpoints frontend-proxy -n astronomy-shop"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-8bb5e24b3700702d', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 981, 'prompt_tokens': 5644, 'total_tokens': 6625, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfbfb-2e7a-7d32-983f-b955aa2a535d-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl get endpoints frontend-proxy -n astronomy-shop'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-8bb5e24b3700702d', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 5644, 'output_tokens': 981, 'total_tokens': 6625}), ToolMessage(content='NAME             ENDPOINTS           AGE\nfrontend-proxy   10.244.0.241:8080   4m27s\n', id='046cd70a-5486-4a8e-bc55-093966e685ac', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-8bb5e24b3700702d'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='7eadc86d-77e5-47e7-8307-71ef2fb0e0c5'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose an issue with the frontend-proxy service in the astronomy-shop namespace. The frontend-proxy pod is in a Terminating state, but the deployment and service configurations seem correct. The previous steps checked the pods, deployments, service YAML, events, and endpoints. The endpoints show that the service is correctly pointing to the pod\'s IP and port.\n\nSince the service is correctly configured and the endpoints are present, the next step is to check if there\'s a network policy blocking traffic to the frontend-proxy. Network policies can restrict communication between services, so if there\'s a policy that\'s too restrictive, it might cause the pod to terminate or prevent proper traffic routing. \n\nThe get_network_policy tool can retrieve the network policies in the namespace. If there\'s a policy that\'s blocking the frontend-proxy, it would explain why the pod is having issues despite the service being correctly set up. Alternatively, if there\'s no network policy, maybe the issue is elsewhere, like a misconfigured Ingress or a problem with the pod\'s own configuration. But since the service is working (endpoints are correct), the network policy is a logical next step to check.\n</think>\n\nget_network_policy  \nThe frontend-proxy service has correct endpoints and configurations, but the pod is still terminating. A restrictive NetworkPolicy might be blocking necessary traffic to or from the frontend-proxy pod, causing it to crash or be evicted. Checking network policies in the namespace will confirm if such restrictions exist.  \n\n<tool_call>\n{"name": "get_network_policy", "arguments": {"namespace": "astronomy-shop"}}\n</tool_call>', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 328, 'prompt_tokens': 4866, 'total_tokens': 5194, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfbfb-5fdc-7731-bffb-659fd25c2fd8-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 4866, 'output_tokens': 328, 'total_tokens': 5194}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='ff0093aa-7921-4601-bd7f-1774fcf8ef6d'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let me try to figure out what's going on with the frontend-proxy pod. The user mentioned that the pod is in a Terminating state, and earlier attempts to get its logs and describe it didn't work. The deployment and service configurations seem correct, and the endpoints are properly set. But the pod keeps terminating. \n\nHmm, maybe there's a network policy blocking the pod. The user tried checking the network policy but the tool isn't available. Wait, the available tools don't include get_network_policy. Oh right, the tools listed don't have that function. So I can't use that. \n\nWait, the user tried using exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd to get events and rollout history but faced issues. The rollout history command wasn't allowed because it's not a read-only command. So maybe I should check other aspects. \n\nSince the service and deployment are okay, maybe the issue is with the pod's own configuration. Let me think about other possible causes. Could there be a problem with the pod's resources? Like CPU or memory limits? The user might need to check the pod's resource usage. \n\nAlternatively, maybe there's a liveness or readiness probe failure. If the probes are misconfigured, the pod might be restarting or terminating. Let me check the pod's description again. Wait, earlier when trying to describe the pod, there was an error saying the pod wasn't found. But the deployment shows it's up-to-date. \n\nWait, maybe the pod is in the process of being replaced. The deployment has a rollout history, but the user can't check it due to tool limitations. Alternatively, maybe the pod is being terminated due to a node issue, like the node being drained or having problems. \n\nBut the user can't check the node status directly. Another angle: the frontend-proxy might be dependent on another service that's down. For example, if it's using a database or another microservice that's not available, it could cause the pod to crash. \n\nBut the user already checked the frontend's traces and found none. Maybe the issue is with the pod's own logs. However, the initial logs command didn't work. Wait, the user tried getting logs for the terminating pod, but maybe the pod is already gone, so the logs aren't available. \n\nAlternatively, maybe there's a ConfigMap or Secret that the frontend-proxy depends on, which is missing or misconfigured. The user could check the ConfigMaps and Secrets in the namespace. \n\nBut the available tools don't include checking ConfigMaps directly. The user can use exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd to get ConfigMaps. Let me try that. \n\nSo, the next step would be to list all ConfigMaps in the namespace to see if any are missing or have issues. If there's a ConfigMap that the frontend-proxy relies on, its absence or incorrect content could cause the pod to fail. \n\nAlternatively, check the pod's YAML to see if there are any annotations or environment variables that might be causing issues. But without the pod's YAML, it's hard to tell. \n\nWait, the user can use kubectl describe pod to get more details, but earlier attempts failed. Maybe the pod was deleted, so the describe command isn't finding it. In that case, the deployment might be rolling out a new pod, but the old one is terminating. \n\nSince the deployment shows READY as 1/1, maybe the new pod is healthy, and the old one is being terminated normally. But the user is concerned about the termination. \n\nAlternatively, maybe there's a bug in the frontend-proxy application itself, causing it to crash. Without logs, it's hard to diagnose. \n\nGiven the tools available, the next best step is to check the ConfigMaps and Secrets in the namespace to ensure they're correctly configured. If that doesn't reveal anything, maybe check the pod's events again or look into resource limits. \n\nBut the user can't check resource limits directly. So, the next tool call should be to get ConfigMaps in the namespace to see if any are missing or have issues.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get configmap -n astronomy-shop"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-8ecd1503d865da88', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 870, 'prompt_tokens': 5854, 'total_tokens': 6724, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfbfb-6f65-76a2-850f-c1f99cfbc0a8-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl get configmap -n astronomy-shop'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-8ecd1503d865da88', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 5854, 'output_tokens': 870, 'total_tokens': 6724}), ToolMessage(content='NAME                                        DATA   AGE\nflagd-config                                1      4m46s\ngrafana                                     2      4m46s\ngrafana-alerting                            0      4m46s\ngrafana-config-dashboards                   1      4m46s\ngrafana-dashboard-apm-dashboard             1      4m46s\ngrafana-dashboard-demo-dashboard            1      4m46s\ngrafana-dashboard-exemplars-dashboard       1      4m45s\ngrafana-dashboard-linux-dashboard           1      4m45s\ngrafana-dashboard-nginx-metrics             1      4m46s\ngrafana-dashboard-opentelemetry-collector   1      4m45s\ngrafana-dashboard-postgresql-dashboard      1      4m45s\ngrafana-dashboard-spanmetrics-dashboard     1      4m45s\ngrafana-datasources                         3      4m45s\nkube-root-ca.crt                            1      5m\nopensearch-config                           1      4m46s\notel-collector-agent                        1      4m46s\npostgresql-init                             1      4m45s\nuser-config                                 1      4m46s\n', id='afafad57-ab4a-45ce-bcb7-65895330ea12', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-8ecd1503d865da88'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='ed5f7005-9033-4056-8c6a-89bc68f40afe')]]
