ontend-proxy -n astronomy-shop -o yaml"}}\n</tool_call>', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 285, 'prompt_tokens': 3582, 'total_tokens': 3867, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfbfa-817e-7bc3-8e65-681005255d71-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 3582, 'output_tokens': 285, 'total_tokens': 3867}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='c9360512-586d-4e61-9ba2-472eebd406a4'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let me try to figure out what's going on here. The user is trying to diagnose an issue with the frontend-proxy service in the astronomy-shop namespace. From the previous steps, we saw that the frontend-proxy pod was in a Terminating state, but when they tried to describe it, it wasn't found. Then, checking the deployment showed that the frontend-proxy deployment has 1/1 replicas ready, which seems normal. \n\nNow, the user is looking at the service definition for frontend-proxy. The service is supposed to route traffic to the pods, so if there's a misconfiguration here, that could cause issues. The next step is to check the service's YAML to see if there are any problems. \n\nWait, the user just called `kubectl get service frontend-proxy -n astronomy-shop -o yaml` and got a response. Let me check the response to see what's in there. The service YAML would include details like the selector, ports, and type. If the selector doesn't match the pod's labels, the service wouldn't route traffic correctly, leading to the pod being terminated or not being accessible. \n\nLooking at the service's YAML, if the selector is incorrect, that's a problem. For example, if the service 