ional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 285, 'prompt_tokens': 3582, 'total_tokens': 3867, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfbfa-817e-7bc3-8e65-681005255d71-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 3582, 'output_tokens': 285, 'total_tokens': 3867}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='c9360512-586d-4e61-9ba2-472eebd406a4'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let me try to figure out what's going on here. The user is trying to diagnose an issue with the frontend-proxy service in the astronomy-shop namespace. From the previous steps, we saw that the frontend-proxy pod was in a Terminating state, but when they tried to describe it, it wasn't found. Then, checking the deployment showed that the frontend-proxy deployment has 1/1 replicas ready, which seems normal. \n\nNow, the user is looking at the service definition for frontend-proxy. The service is supposed to route traffic to the pods, so if there's a misconfiguration here, that could cause issues. The next step is to check the service's YAML to see if there are any problems. \n\nWait, the user just called `kubectl get service frontend-proxy -n astronomy-shop -o yaml` and got a response. Let me check the response to see what's in there. The service YAML would include details like the selector, ports, and type. If the selector doesn't match the pod's labels, the service wouldn't route traffic correctly, leading to the pod being terminated or not being accessible. \n\nLooking at the service's YAML, if the selector is incorrect, that's a problem. For example, if the service is looking for a label like `app: frontend-proxy` but the pod has a different label, the service wouldn't find the pods, leading to termination. Also, checking the ports to ensure they match the container's exposed ports is important. \n\nAnother thing to check is the service type. If it's set to ClusterIP but the pods are not reachable, that could cause issues. Or if there's a misconfiguration in the endpoints. \n\nOnce the service YAML is reviewed, if any discrepancies are found, that would be the root cause. If not, maybe the issue is elsewhere, like in the deployment configuration or network policies. But since the pod was terminating, the service misconfiguration is a likely candidate. \n\nSo, the next step after getting the service YAML is to analyze it for any mismatches or errors. If everything looks good, then the problem might be in another area, like the pod's own configuration or external factors. But based on the current information, checking the service's YAML is the logical next step.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get service frontend-proxy -n astronomy-shop -o yaml"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-9e13c0ce6cee7111', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 501, 'prompt_tokens': 4574, 'total_tokens': 5075, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfbfa-8ed4-7e80-89f3-1825e4b4d61e-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl get service frontend-proxy -n astronomy-shop -o yaml'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-9e13c0ce6cee7111', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 4574, 'output_tokens': 501, 'total_tokens': 5075}), ToolMessage(content='apiVersion: v1\nkind: Service\nmetadata:\n  annota