ck the pod\'s labels. Wait, the user hasn\'t checked the pod\'s labels yet. If the service selector doesn\'t match the pod\'s labels, the service wouldn\'t route traffic properly, but that might not cause the pod to terminate. \n\nWait, the pod is terminating, but the deployment is healthy. Maybe the termination is part of a normal rollout. However, if the frontend-proxy is critical, its termination could affect other services. The user should check if there are any errors in the pod\'s logs before it terminated. Since the pod is already terminating, maybe the logs are no longer available. \n\nAlternatively, maybe there\'s a resource issue, like memory or CPU limits. The user hasn\'t checked the resource usage. Using `kubectl top pod` could show if the pod was exceeding limits. But the user hasn\'t done that yet. \n\nWait, the user has a tool called `get_metrics` which can query Prometheus. Maybe they can check if there are any metrics related to the frontend-proxy pod, like error rates or resource usage. \n\nBut given the tools available, the next step would be to check the pod\'s logs before it terminated. However, since the pod is already gone, maybe the logs are not available. Alternatively, check the deployment\'s rollout history to see if there were any failures. \n\nWait, the user can use `kubectl rollout history` on the deployment to see if there were any failed rollouts. That might indicate if the termination was due to a deployment issue. \n\nSo, the next tool call should be to check the deployment\'s rollout history. Let me see, the available tools include `exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd`, so the command would be `kubectl rollout history deployment/frontend-proxy -n astronomy-shop`.\n</think>\n\n<tool_call>\n{"name": "exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd", "arguments": {"command": "kubectl rollout history deployment/frontend-proxy -n astronomy-shop"}}\n</tool_call>', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata