Map named \'frontend-proxy\'. So if the frontend-proxy deployment is referencing a ConfigMap that doesn\'t exist, that could cause the pod to fail.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the frontend-proxy is using a ConfigMap that\'s correctly named but has incorrect data. For example, if the ConfigMap has a key that\'s expected but missing, the application might crash. Since the user can\'t check the ConfigMap\'s contents directly, maybe they need to check the deployment\'s YAML to see which ConfigMap is being used. But without that, it\'s hard to tell.\n\nAnother possibility: the frontend-proxy is using a ConfigMap that\'s not in the same namespace, leading to a failure. Or maybe the ConfigMap is in a different namespace, causing the pod to not find it. But the user hasn\'t checked that yet.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the frontend-proxy is using a ConfigMap that\'s correctly named but the data is incorrect. For example, if the ConfigMap has a key that\'s supposed to be a URL or a token, but the value is missing or wrong, the application would fail. Since the user can\'t check the ConfigMap\'s contents, they might need to look into the pod\'s logs again, but the pod is already terminating. However, the user tried to get the logs and it wasn\'t available.\n\nWait, the user tried to describe the pod, but it was not found. Maybe the pod was already deleted, so the logs aren\'t available. So the next step is to check if the ConfigMap is correctly set up. If the frontend-proxy is using a ConfigMap that\'s missing, that\'s a root cause. Alternatively, maybe the ConfigMap is present but has incorrect data.\n\nSince the user can\'t check the ConfigMap\'s contents directly, maybe they should check the deployment\'s YAML to see which ConfigMap is being used. But since the user can\'t run `kubectl describe deployment`, they might need to use another approach. Alternatively, check the ConfigMaps again to see if any are related to the frontend-proxy. The user might have missed a ConfigMap with 