s and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='e25af516-aa53-4de9-ad28-a1a78304e544'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose an issue with the frontend-proxy pod in the astronomy-shop namespace. The pod is in a Terminating state, but when they tried to describe it, it wasn\'t found. Then they checked the deployment and service configs, which seemed okay. They looked at events and saw scaling actions, but no errors. Now, they tried to check the rollout history, but the tool doesn\'t support that command.\n\nHmm, the available read-only commands include \'kubectl get\' and \'kubectl describe\'. Since \'kubectl describe pod\' failed earlier, maybe the pod was already deleted, so it\'s not there anymore. But the deployment shows it\'s up-to-date. Wait, maybe the pod is in the process of being replaced. The user saw two scaling actions: one for version 84788b44b and then 6df8c768f5. That suggests a rollout. But the current pod is in Terminating, so maybe there\'s a new pod being created. \n\nSince the rollout history isn\'t accessible, maybe the next step is to check the current pods of the deployment. The user already ran \'kubectl get pods\' and saw the frontend-proxy-84788b44b-gtgdz in Terminating. But maybe there\'s another pod in the deployment that\'s not shown. Wait, the deployment\'s READY is 1/1, so maybe the new pod is already running. Alternatively, maybe the old pod is being terminated, and the new one is taking over. \n\nBut the user needs to confirm if there\'s an error in the pod\'s logs. However, the pod is already terminating, so logs might not be available. Alternatively, check the current pods again to see if the Terminating pod is the only one. If the deployment has multiple replicas, maybe there\'s another pod running. Wait, the deployment\'s UP-TO-DATE is 1, and AVAILABLE is 1, so it\'s healthy. \n\nWait, maybe the issue is that the frontend-proxy service is misconfigured. The service\'s selector is based on opentelemetry.io/name: frontend-proxy. But if the pods don\'t have that label, the service can\'t find them. But earlier checks showed the pod was part of the deployment, so maybe the labels are correct. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the frontend-proxy is not properly routing traffic, causing issues. But the user hasn\'t checked the service\'s endpoints yet. The service\'s endpoints might not be correctly pointing to the pods. So checking the endpoints could help. \n\nThe available tools include \'get_services\' from Grafana, but the user already listed the services. Maybe checking the service\'s endpoints with kubectl get endpoints. But the user can\'t use \'kubectl get endpoints\' directly. Wait, the available read-only commands include \'kubectl get\', so they can run \'kubectl get endpoints frontend-proxy -n astronomy-shop\'. That would show if the service has endpoints. If there are no endpoints, it might indicate a problem with the service\'s selector or pod labels. \n\nSo the next step is to check the endpoints for the frontend-proxy service to see if it\'s correctly pointing to the pods. If there\'s no endpoint, that could be the root cause. Let me check that.\n</think>\n\nexec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \nTo verify if the frontend-proxy service is correctly routing traffic to its pods, I will check the endpoints associated with the service. If there are no endpoints, it could indicate a misconfiguration in the service\'s selector or pod labels, preventing proper communication.  \n\n<tool_call>\n{"name": "exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd", "arguments": {"command": "kubectl get endpoints frontend-proxy -n astronomy-shop"}}\n</tool_call>', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 767, 'prompt_tokens': 4650, 'total_tokens': 5417, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'sto