which should resolve to the otel-collector service. But if that service isn\'t available, it might cause the container to fail.\n\nAnother thing to check is the resource limits. The container has a memory limit of 65Mi. If the application is using more memory than that, it could be getting evicted. But since the pod is in a Terminating state, maybe it was evicted due to memory pressure. Let me check the metrics for the frontend-proxy pod to see if there were any memory issues. \n\nWait, I can use the get_metrics tool to query Prometheus for memory usage metrics. Let me try that. The query would be something like "container_memory_usage_bytes{container=\\"frontend-proxy\\", namespace=\\"astronomy-shop\\"}" to get the memory usage. If the value exceeds the limit, that\'s a problem. \n\nAlternatively, maybe there\'s an error in the container\'s startup process. If the container is crashing immediately, the logs might not be available once it\'s terminated. But since the pod is in Terminating state, maybe the logs are still accessible. Let me try to get the logs again, but this time using the current pod name. Wait, the pod might have been deleted, so the logs might not be there anymore. \n\nHmm, maybe the issue is with the service account. The deployment uses the service account "astronomy-shop". Let me check if that service account has the necessary permissions. But I don\'t have a tool for checking service account permissions. \n\nWait, the user mentioned that the frontend-proxy is part of the OpenTelemetry Demo, so maybe there\'s a specific configuration or dependency that\'s missing. For example, the OTEL_COLLECTOR_HOST might be pointing to a service that\'s not running or has incorrect endpoints. Let me check the otel-collector service to see if it\'s up. \n\nBut I already checked the services in the namespace, and the otel-collector-agent is running. So that\'s probably not the issue. \n\nAnother angle: the frontend-proxy pod might be terminating due to a liveness probe failure. Let me check the deployment\'s probe configurations. In the deployment YAML, under the containers section, there\'s a livenessProbe or readinessProbe? Wait, looking at the deployment spec, I don\'t see any probe configurations listed. If there are no probes, the pod might not be restarting on failures, leading to termination. \n\nWait, the deployment\'s strategy is RollingUpdate with maxSurge and maxUnavailable. If the pod is failing to start, the deployment might be replacing it, but if the new pod also fails, it could get stuck. However, the current status shows that the deployment is available and the pod is in Terminating state. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the pod is being terminated by the cluster due to some resource constraints. Let me check the metrics for CPU and memory usage. Using get_metrics, I can query for the container\'s memory and CPU usage. If the memory usage is consistently high, that could be the cause. \n\nSo, the next step is to use the get_metrics tool to check the memory and CPU usage of the frontend-proxy container. If there\'s an anomaly, that could indicate the root cause. Let me proceed with that.\n</think>\n\nget_metrics  \nThe frontend-proxy pod is in a "Terminating" state, and previous checks didn\'t reveal immediate issues. To determine if resource exhaustion (e.g., memory or CPU) is causing the pod to be evicted or crash, I will query real-time metrics for memory and CPU usage of the frontend-proxy container. This will help identify if the pod is exceeding its resource limits or experiencing unexpected resource pressure.  \n\n<tool_call>\n{"name": "get_metrics", "arguments": {"query": "container_memory_usage_bytes{container=\\"frontend-proxy\\", namespace=\\"astronomy-shop\\"}"}}\n</tool_call>', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 1068, 'prompt_tokens': 7897, 'total_tokens': 8965, 'completion_tokens_details': None,