yment has a rollout history, but the user can't check it due to tool limitations. Alternatively, maybe the pod is being terminated due to a node issue, like the node being drained or having problems. \n\nBut the user can't check the node status directly. Another angle: the frontend-proxy might be dependent on another service that's down. For example, if it's using a database or another microservice that's not available, it could cause the pod to crash. \n\nBut the user already checked the frontend's traces and found none. Maybe the issue is with the pod's own logs. However, the initial logs command didn't work. Wait, the user tried getting logs for the terminating pod, but maybe the pod is already gone, so the logs aren't available. \n\nAlternatively, maybe there's a ConfigMap or Secret that the frontend-proxy depends on, which is missing or misconfigured. The user could check the ConfigMaps and Secrets in the namespace. \n\nBut the available tools don't include checking ConfigMaps directly. The user can use exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd to get ConfigMaps. Let me try that. \n\nSo, the next step would be to list all ConfigMaps in the namespace to see if any are missing or have issues. If there's a ConfigMap that the frontend-proxy relies on, its absence or incorrect content could cause the pod to fail. \n\nAlternatively, check the pod's YAML to see if there are any annotations or environment variables that might be causing issues. But without the pod's YAML, it's hard to tell. \n\nWait, the user can use kubectl describe pod to get more details, but earlier attempts failed. Maybe the pod was deleted, so the describe command isn't finding it. In that case, the deployment might be rolling out a new pod, but the old one is terminating. \n\nSince the deployment shows READY as 1/1, maybe the new pod is healthy, and the old one is being terminated normally. But the user is concerned about the termination. \n\nAlternatively, maybe there's a bug in the frontend-proxy application itself, causing it to crash. Without logs, it's hard to diagnose. \n\nGiven the tools available, the next best step is to check the ConfigMaps and Secrets in the namespace to ensure they're correctly configured. If that doesn't reveal anything, maybe check the pod's events again or look into resource limits. \n\nBut the user can't check resource limits directly. So, the next tool call should be to get ConfigMaps in the namespace to see if any are missing or have issues.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get configmap -n astronomy-shop"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-8ecd1503d865da88', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 870, 'prompt_tokens': 5854, 'total_tokens': 6724, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfbfb-6f65-76a2-850f-c1f99cfbc0a8-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl get configmap -n astronomy-shop'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-8ecd1503d865da88', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 5854, 'output_tokens': 870, 'total_tokens': 6724}), ToolMessage(content='NAME                                        DATA   AGE\nflagd-config                                1      4m46s\ngrafana                                     2      4m46s\ngrafana-alerting                            0      4m46s\ngrafana-config-dashboards                   1      4m46s\ngrafana-dashboard-apm-dashboard             1      4m46s\ngrafana-dashboard-demo-dashboard            1      4m46s\ngrafana-dashboard-exemplars-dashboard       1      4m45s\ngrafana-dashboard-linux-dashboard           1      4m45s\ngrafana-dashboard-nginx-metrics             1   