there\'s an issue with pulling it. But the deployment shows the imagePullPolicy as IfNotPresent, so it might be using a cached image.\n\nAnother angle: the pod\'s termination could be due to an error in the container itself. Let me check the container logs again. If the pod is terminating, maybe there\'s an error message in the logs that I can see. Let me try to get the logs for the frontend-proxy pod again, even though it\'s terminating. If the pod is already gone, the logs might still be available. \n\nWait, the user tried to describe the pod earlier and got a "not found" error. Maybe the pod was deleted, and the deployment is trying to recreate it. Let me check the deployment\'s rollout history to see if there\'s a recent update that might have caused the issue. But the user tried to use kubectl rollout history, which isn\'t a read-only command. Hmm, maybe I can check the deployment\'s status again. The deployment shows availableReplicas as 1, so it\'s currently running. But the pod is in terminating state. \n\nWait, the pod might be in the process of being replaced by a new replica. Let me check the pods again. The user previously listed the pods, and frontend-proxy-84788b44b-gtgdz is terminating, while frontend-proxy-6df8c768f5-pj4kn is running. So the deployment is rolling out a new version. Maybe the new pod is having issues. Let me check the logs of the new pod to see if there are any errors. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the termination is due to a liveness probe failure. Let me check the pod\'s configuration for liveness and readiness probes. The deployment\'s template might have probes set. If the liveness probe is failing, the pod would be restarted, but if it\'s not recovering, it might be terminated. Let me check the deployment\'s YAML for any probe configurations. \n\nLooking back at the deployment YAML, the containers section has env variables but no mention of probes. So maybe there are no probes set, and the pod is terminating for another reason. \n\nAnother possibility: the pod is being terminated due to an external factor, like a network issue or a misconfigured service. Since the service has endpoints, the network configuration seems okay. \n\nWait, the user tried to get the metrics for CPU and memory, but the query returned empty results. That might indicate that the metrics aren\'t being collected for the frontend-proxy container. Maybe the OpenTelemetry collector isn\'t properly configured, leading to missing metrics. But the service account and security context seem okay. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the pod is terminating because of a missing dependency. For example, if the frontend-proxy relies on another service like flagd or grafana, and those services are down, it could cause the frontend-proxy to fail. Let me check the status of those services. \n\nThe user previously listed the pods, and services like flagd, grafana, and jaeger are running. So dependencies might be okay. \n\nHmm, I\'m running out of options. Let me try to get the logs of the terminating pod again. If the pod is still in the terminating state, maybe the logs are still accessible. Let me attempt that.\n</think>\n\nexec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \nThe frontend-proxy pod is in a "Terminating" state, and previous checks did not reveal resource exhaustion or configuration issues. To determine the exact reason for termination, I will retrieve the logs of the terminating pod. These logs may contain error messages or crash details that indicate the root cause, such as application failures, crashes, or external issues.  \n\n<tool_call>\n{"name": "exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd", "arguments": {"command": "kubectl logs frontend-proxy-84788b44b-gtgdz -n astronomy-shop --previous"}}\n</tool_call>', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 1039, 'prompt_tokens': 8130, 'total_tokens': 9169, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'mod