They also checked the service configuration, endpoints, and network policies, but nothing seemed amiss. Metrics queries for CPU and memory didn't show any issues. The deployment's status showed it was progressing, which is normal during a rollout. 

Since all the checks came back clean and the pod's termination was part of a planned rollout, the root cause was determined to be a normal Kubernetes deployment process. No faults were found, so the user submitted the answer indicating that the termination was intentional and part of the rollout.

